WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.248
When a Union law and a State

2
00:00:02.248 --> 00:00:04.674
law clash, the Union usually wins.

3
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:05.447
Usually.

4
00:00:05.772 --> 00:00:08.133
Article 246 splits law-making

5
00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:09.517
into three lists:

6
00:00:09.934 --> 00:00:12.234
Union, State, and Concurrent.

7
00:00:12.553 --> 00:00:14.586
A subject on no list belongs

8
00:00:14.586 --> 00:00:16.983
to Parliament, under Article 248.

9
00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:18.878
If a State law clashes with

10
00:00:18.878 --> 00:00:21.223
Parliament's law on a Concurrent subject,

11
00:00:21.783 --> 00:00:23.322
the State law is void to

12
00:00:23.322 --> 00:00:24.091
that extent.

13
00:00:24.526 --> 00:00:26.623
Unless the President assents to it.

14
00:00:26.901 --> 00:00:28.834
Then it prevails in that State,

15
00:00:29.282 --> 00:00:31.296
until Parliament legislates again.

16
00:00:31.607 --> 00:00:34.328
And Article 356, President's Rule?

17
00:00:34.669 --> 00:00:36.735
In Bommai, in 1994, nine

18
00:00:36.735 --> 00:00:40.091
judges made it open to judicial review,

19
00:00:40.678 --> 00:00:42.488
with majority tested on the floor.

20
00:00:43.051 --> 00:00:44.740
The lists divide power.

21
00:00:45.041 --> 00:00:46.800
The courts guard the balance.

22
00:00:47.177 --> 00:00:47.694
Your turn.

23
00:00:47.949 --> 00:00:50.113
Who holds powers over subjects

24
00:00:50.113 --> 00:00:50.906
on no list?
